August 20, 2016.

I sat down thinking I might make the three simultaneous strobes consume a
constant amount of horizontal space per unit time, so that they remain in
synch.  That way, you can easily see how a particular moment in time
matches across the different octaves.

I realized the problem is it will introduce aliasing.  No matter how I do
it, in order to have ratios that are more complicated than 1:2 or 1:4, I
will have to have different columns representing different weights of
input signal.  That will result, visually, in aliasing.  That's just not
acceptable.

And while I'm at it, taking scrolling mode off my todo list.  It would be
nice, but it really doesn't make a difference, and it definitely will
increase the required graphics bandwidth by around 100x.  Probably, that
is no big deal.  But somewhere, it will be a big deal, so why do it.


August 21, 2016.

Cleaning up some more long-standing TODOs...  A freq adjustable by
+/- 0.1Hz is easy to do and might be useful to someone who actually
adjusts the A freq at all.

But expressing the stretch factor in cents per octave is a bit of a
conundrum for me.

The current stretch factor counts in terms of
   (2 + stretch*0.00025) ^ note
Doing cents, instead, would be more like
   2 ^ (note + stretch/1200)
For note=1 and stretch=1, we're getting:
   2.00025 (old)
   2.00116 (new, cents/octave)

So I think it would be cool to do cents/octave, but if I'm understanding
correctly then I'd really want to do 0.1 cents/octave:
   2 ^ (1 + stretch/12000) = 2.00012

That's probably not hard to do, or to explain.  The positive is that it
will give a concrete reference for people who know about cents, and that
seems pretty big to me.  The negative is that some users probably have a
setting today that they like, and do not expect its meaning to change
under them.  We can detect an old setting and convert it to the closest
equivalent of the new setting.....

A thorough derivation of the octave stretch to cents_per_octave (which
will be *10 and actually representing milis_per_octave):
   c=cents-4800 (hope this cancels out)
   os=octave_stretch*0.00001
   cpo=cents_per_octave/10
   freq=440 * ((2+os))^(c/1200)
   freq=440 * 2^(c*(1+(cpo/1200))/1200)
      equate:
   440 * ((2+os))^(c/1200) = 440 * 2^(c*(1+(cpo/1200))/1200)
   ((2+os))^(c/1200) = 2^(c*(1+(cpo/1200))/1200)
      lg of both sides:
   lg(((2+os))^(c/1200)) = c*(1+(cpo/1200))/1200
      divide both sides by lg(2+os) (synthetic log_{2+os}(x)):
   c/1200 = c*(1+(cpo/1200))/(1200*lg(2+os))
      multiply by 1200*lg(2+os):
   c*lg(2+os) = c*(1+(cpo/1200))
   lg(2+os) = 1+(cpo/1200)
   lg(2+os)-1 = cpo/1200
   cpo = (lg(2+os)-1)*1200
   cents_per_octave = (lg(2+octave_stretch*0.00001)-1)*12000;

So the smallest step is octave_stretch +/- 25:
   cents_per_octave = (lg(2.00025)-1)*12000 =  2.16391
   cents_per_octave = (lg(1.99975)-1)*12000 = -2.16418
It's not actually symmetric around 0, but it is close enough, and anyways
the actual stretching is symmetric around 0 even if this conversion is
not.

It seems like it should be pretty reasonable, even rounded to int.

...

Well, the tone generator worked pretty much as designed.  It seems to
have a slight vibrato, and it also seems to have a high pitched overtone
that is pronounced when playing low notes (octave 2).  That's likely to
be psychosomatic based on its low volume and background noise..

But more significantly, when the frequency is very high (octave 6), the
display says it is a little out of tune.  It is possible that these
higher frequencies simply exaggerate the out-of-tune-ness of the
hardware, which is actually visible even in octave 4.  But it's also
possible that the rounding error from my integer t/delta_t in
ToneGenerator are becoming visible.

So, I'll want to figure out what the deal is with both of those, and then
release!

...

I played with octave 6 a bit, and it seems to behave identically whether
T_MULT is 1024 (current) or 16384.  And truly, 1974Hz would yield an
error under +/-0.0013 Hz with T_MULT=1024.  Which would take around 1000
seconds to roll around entirely, while the error I'm seeing is rolling
around in about 10 seconds.

So that makes me wonder if the rounding error isn't in the strobe itself.
Not sure what step there would introduce it, though...

...

Just thinking about it, I found the rounding error in the strobe.  I
think its key value is msamps_per_col.  For 1974Hz, we're looking at
around 22.340 samples per column.  If that was 22.341, instead, we get
+/-0.088 Hz, which is in the ballpark of our observation...

So we should probably switch to, i.e., usamps (microsamples), but we
really do have to make sure we've got plenty of range to cover low
frequencies.  Absolute minimum seems to be 27.5Hz, or 1603 samples per
column.  That's cutting it pretty close for a 32-bit int, but if we use
unsigned and I check how it's accumulated, it might work.  At any rate,
we really only need on the order of 1/100,000th of a sample.


August 27, 2016.

Conservatively, we have to assume the screen is up to 4000 pixels wide
and the samples per column is up to 2000 pixels (22Hz).  Unfortunately,
the current implementation multiplies width by msamps_per_col, yielding a
maximum number of sub-samples of 536 -- less than the current 1000!
Meaning if someone tuned the lowest note on a 4k screen, they might see
some weird overflow-aliasing!

I think the solution is to avoid multiplying by the width...then we have
a maximum number of fractional samples around 2,147,483, which is much
more than the 100,000 I was aiming for.

That doesn't seem like it should be hard, but it's definitely more work
than the s/1000/100000/ that I was planning on.

...

I think I got the xsamps_per_col to work, and now the tone display seems
pretty solid.

However, one thing I notice is that there is an aliasing effect already,
especially pronounced at higher frequencies.  I'm not sure, but I think
it is caused by making the vertical height be constant.  If one sample
contributes to more than one vertical pixel, then this aliasing is almost
guaranteed.

So that makes me wonder if making the horizontal speed constant would
really introduce that much new aliasing.


June 4, 2017.

Ben Forsberg has given the suggestion to make the FFT display be
logarithmic in frequency.  It seems like a winner to me.

But sitting down to do it, I'm not sure where to start exactly.

The way it currently works is that there are N/2 bins, which are
spread linearly across a virtual x coordinate that is
[0..FFT_RANGE_MULT).  fft_offset and fft_width define the current
viewport in that coordinate space.  The frequency of a bin is
bin_index*samplerate/fft_size.  So the bins cover frequencies of
[0..samplerate/2), regardless of how many bins there are.
   x(bin) = bin*FFT_RANGE_MULT/tot_bins

I want to spread the same bins logarithmically across the same
virtual x coordinate space.  So in both modes, we need to preserve
  x(0) = 0
  x(N/2) = FFT_RANGE_MULT

I think it's going to be some sort of x(bin) = c*log(a*bin+b)+d

Let's try first with b=1 (so logarithm is always positive), and a=1
(*shrug*).
   x(bin) = c*log(bin+1)+d
   x(0) = 0 = c*log(1)+d
   d=0
   x(bin) = c*log(bin+1)
   x(N/2) = FFT_RANGE_MULT = c*log(N/2+1)
   c = FFT_RANGE_MULT/log(N/2+1)
   x(bin) = log(bin+1)*FFT_RANGE_MULT/log(N/2+1)

That wasn't so hard, and I like that there is a single right answer.

Will be useful to know that backwards, probably...
   x = log(bin+1)*FFT_RANGE_MULT/log(N/2+1)
   x*log(N/2+1)/FFT_RANGE_MULT = log(bin+1)
   exp(x*log(N/2+1)/FFT_RANGE_MULT) = bin+1
   bin(x) = exp(x*log(N/2+1)/FFT_RANGE_MULT)-1
   bin(x) = (N/2+1)^(x/FFT_RANGE_MULT)-1

I think everything will fall out in a fairly straightforward manner
except for the drawing itself, which is somewhat difficult because the
number of bins per pixel will change....


October 29, 2017.

I have to push out a new release because Android 8 (Oreo) discontinued
hasAlpha=false without any documentation or API change.  Google kind of
sucks.

Anyways, that means I have to shit or get off the pot with regards to the
logarithmic mode in the FFT.  I really am sold that it is a good idea,
but I'm not motivated to puzzle it out right now.  So I'm checking it in
as a "log_fft" branch, and gonna strip it away from the main branch.


