March 4, 2018.

I now get weekly emails from new people telling me the octave starts at
C.  It is my fault, because I didn't push out a new release after
implementing that...

Anyways, having looked again in the Reblitz book, it occurs to me it
might be appreciated to put the piano key number as well.  That is,
A-4 440Hz: A49.

And also, I got an email from M. Caldwell saying his Galaxy J3 Prime does
not like version 10 - it displays a blank screen, which is the problem on
most phones that version 10 was supposed to fix.  I sent him version 1.8
and he says it works for him.

Version 10 has a work-around for a pervasive Android 8 Oreo bug where
c.drawBitmap() does not honor hasAlpha=false.  I worked around it by
providing an 0xFF000000 alpha channel, but still using hasAlpha=false.
It sounds like this one Samsung phone has a problem with hasAlpha=false
when a non-zero alpha channel is provided anyways.  I think that is
stupid, but I think we can probably work around it by simply setting
hasAlpha=true, then it will work everywhere!  Hah, I jest.


And I'm gonna punt on the other improvements (constant horizontal speed,
log_fft) just so people can enjoy the new octave starts.

...

I happened to look at the play store and find 4 distinct crashes had been
reported against version 10, so I'd like to take care of them.

The obvious crash is a race case on holder, fixed in commit dd6d7ea.

Tempering also had gotten a little fubared, and I fixed the crash from
apply_temper() working on negative cents in commit 8cfa260.

And wakelock.release() sometimes fails, just stabbing in the dark with
commit 7cd7a05.

But the real odd one is an IllegalArgumentException thrown from
android.media.AudioRecord.audioBuffSizeCheck (AudioRecord.java:751),
which is called from the "new AudioRecord" call.  Most likely
getMinBufferSize() is returning a negative number (error code), which I'm
guessing means that 44100 isn't supported.  There is a single report of
this, and it is on a "Galaxy Tab S2", and there is at least one report on
stackoverflow of a "Galaxy Tab" that didn't want to initialize its
AudioRecord.  In that report, they were unable to find a rate that did
work, but they did not try 48000.

If it becomes a bigger problem, we should probably try a variety of rates
and make the rate variable.  But for now, I think it's just a single
oddball.


March 12, 2018.

Ben Hemmendinger has two requests.  First is a black-and-white or
selectable-color mode to help people work around red-green
color-blindness.  That's a no-brainer, just a UI question.

The other is more interesting.  He wants "presets" like for the 6
specific strings on his guitar, so he doesn't need to do semi+ 5 (or 4)
times between strings, and he's got some zany tempering for a guitar!
So it's a tedious process for him to tune each of the strings, and if he
wants to nail this designed temper, he'll want to tune each string with
the strobe, rather than doing most by ear.

I like the idea because it obviates "temper" -- that's just a preset list
that happens to have 12 entries.  And because it's better than the "hold
down semi+/- to go by 5ths", which isn't actually what you want for a
guitar anyways, in addition to being kind of obscure.

So I could make a catch-all normal android preferences activity (hanging
off the menu), which would have the color setting.  Then I could throw
"harmonics" in there, because there's no advantage to seeing the results
of that in real time (IMO).

Then I could replace the "temper" panel with a "presets" panel.  Its 3
columns of buttons would be:

   note+/-    circulate through the presets
   oct+/-     an octave bias to presets, so 12 presets is a complete temper
   select     brings up preset-selection screen

The preset-selection screen would be a list of the presets, with a button
at the bottom for "new".  Long-tap a preset for context menu of
edit/delete.

Editing a preset (new or existing) would be a list of the notes in the
preset, with a button at the bottom for "new".  Long-tap a note for
context menu of edit/delete.

Editing a note in a preset is a question.  I could go into a strobe mode
like the "note" panel, with an "OK" button.  Or I could just use the
Android UI to select note/octave/cents.  Note would probably be an C-B
dropdown, a checkbox for sharp.  Octave would be 1-8 dropdown.  Cents
would be numeric entry (TextView).  That would be simple, and it would
fit in probably a Dialog sort of thing, and I think most people arranging
presets don't mind thinking about what they really want off-line.
I think everyone knows a guitar is E/A/D/G/B/E over two full octaves, for
example.


March 24, 2018.

The unknown for presets is how to store them.  I want to stick with the
simple native SharedPreferences model - key/value, where value is either
int or String.  I think I will make a "magic ID" (probably just
monotonically-incrementing integers) for each new preset, and put a list
of those in a string with key "presets".  Then each preset will have a
"preset_<ID>_name" for its string name, and "preset_<ID>_notes" which
will be a comma-separated list of cents values (4800=middle A).

So, i.e.
   presets:0
   preset_0_name:Major scale
   preset_0_notes:3900,4100,4300,4400,4600,4800,5000
   ...

Should be easy.  Go ahead and load them all at once and save them all at
once just like the rest of the settings.


September 1, 2018.

Dan McClung reported: "I keep getting notices that the microphone is
running in the background and lowering the battery".

It seems to me like TunerTime.onPause() kills Strobe kills Listener calls
AudioRecord.release() so I don't really understand what could be failing.
I instrumented it (System.out.println) and it is definitely calling into
AudioRecord.release() when it's supposed to.  It also sets recorder=null
so memory allocation should be able to clean up the reference as well.

So I think the user is experiencing something unusual, and I'm not overly
concerned about it.  The only change I can think of to do is to ensure
AudioRecord.release() is called even if AudioRecord.stop() threw an
exception, but really I don't think that's a big concern...


