December 27, 2020.

A couple users have reported that it doesn't let them access the
microphone, but they can go into settings -> apps & notifications ->
advanced -> permission manager and manually enable it, then it works.
I think upgrading the SDK caused Android to stop grand-fathering in the
permission from the manifest, and now it has to be requested explicitly.
It "works" for old users because the fact that grand-fathering happened
already is treated as implicit consent to the permission.  So only users
who installed it new since October have this problem.

Apparently it's been since SDK 23 (MARSHMALLOW), "dangerous permissions"
need runtime interactive authorization.  In the Manifest.permission
reference, it says:

   public static final String RECORD_AUDIO
   Allows an application to record audio.
   Protection level: dangerous

So that settles that.  The other permission is WAKE_LOCK:

   public static final String WAKE_LOCK
   Allows using PowerManager WakeLocks to keep processor from sleeping or
   screen from dimming.
   Protection level: normal

So I guess I don't need to worry about that one.  Normal ones are
"install-time permissions", they are authorized at install-time, I guess.

Just gonna follow the directions from Google's "request app permissions"
document.

...

ugh, another example of Android being stupid.  They make you request the
permission and then it onPause()s your app, then when the dialog is done
(or it is short-circuited by always-deny), it onResume()s your app, then
it sends an onRequestPermissionsResult().  The problem is, the permission
check is in onResume(), because they told me to put the permission check
where it is used, and onResume() is what calls start_strobe().

A lot of people on stackoverflow recommend putting the permission check
in onCreate() instead, which seems wrong to me.  I was about to make a
state machine to avoid checking the permission if we haven't heard back
from the last permission request yet, but I found someone recommending
onStart(), which seems more appropriate.

From reading the lifecycle document again, I think my take is correct
that onCreate() is called before the activity has display focus, so it
seems a poor place to do a check that will have UI consequences.
onStart() is called when the activity first gains display focus, but not
again if it is paused/resumed.  Sounds good to me.


